{Against K. W. Lloyd in the Premier Reserves Major, M. Vasiljevlc. of
Jugoslavia, play­ing White, turned a French Defence into a sort of Inverted
King's Indian Defence. Imagina­tively. Lloyd saw that 5...e5 (he could have
got there In one move instead of two) is no waste of time because of White's
restrained opening play. He then, justifiably, accepted split pawns for the
advantage of retaining two bishops against two knights. With both players
rather short of time on their clocks, Lloyd staked all on a break-through on
the king’s rook file which succeeded brilliantly. Vaslljevic rather losing
his nerve in the time scramble. A typical K. W. Lloyd game, won by hard,
persistent pressure rather than fireworks.} 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 3. Nd2 Nc6 4. c3
Nf6 5. Ngf3 e5 6. Be2 dxe4 7. dxe4 Bc5 8. O-O a5 9. Qc2 O-O 10. Nc4 Qe7 11. Bg5
h6 12. Bh4 Rd8 13. a4 Bg4 14. Rfd1 Qe6 15. Ncd2 g5 16. Bc4 Qe7 17. Bg3 Nh5 18.
Bd5 Nxg3 19. hxg3 Rd7 20. Bxc6 bxc6 21. Nc4 f6 22. Rxd7 Bxd7 23. Rd1 Be6 24.
Qd3 Qf7 25. Nfd2 Be7 26. Qe2 Kg7 27. Ne3 Bc8 28. Ndc4 Be6 29. g4 Bc5 30. g3
Bxe3 31. Nxe3 Rh8 32. Qa6 h5 33. Nf5+ Kg6 34. gxh5+ Rxh5 35. Qxc6 Qh7 36. Qe8+
{(Score says 'Q-B8ch')} Bf7 37. Qd8 Rh1+ 38. Kg2 Rxd1 39. Ne7+ ({JS note:
simply} 39. Qxd1 {looks very good. Score problem?}) 39... Kh5 40. Qc8 ({
JS note: hard to believe White missed} 40. Qxd1+ Kh6 (40... g4 41. Qh1+ Kg5 42.
Qxh7 {wins}) 41. Nf5+ Kg6 42. Qd7 {which gives White a safe winning advantage.
Maybe there was a problem with the score?}) 40... Qxe4+ {Source: Birmingham
Daily Post, 8 January 1959 - BH Wood column} 0-1

